Q: Descriptor concept in NIC A: <p>The short answer:</p>
<ol>
<li><p>They are software constructs that follows the definition by NIC hardware, so both understands and could talk to each other.</p>
</li>
<li><p>They could get filled either way, depending on the contract defined by the vendor. Possible scenarios could include but are not limited to:</p>
</li>
</ol>
<ul>
<li><p>By driver (e.g., for empty buffer prepared by driver to be received by hardware Rx; For packet buffer prepared by driver to be transmitted by hardware Tx)</p>
</li>
<li><p>By the NIC (e.g., for packet buffer written back by hardware for completed Rx packets; for the finished Tx packet buffer indicated by hardware that it's transmitted)</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>More Architectural Details:</strong></p>
<p>Note: I assume you have knowledge of ring data structure, concept of DMA:</p>
<ul>
<li><p><em><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Circular_buffer" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Circular buffer</a></em></p>
</li>
<li><p><em><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Direct_memory_access" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Direct memory access</a></em></p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>A descriptor, as its name implies, describes a packet. It doesn't usually directly contain packet data (but could, in case of inlined data, e.g. for <a href="https://docs.nvidia.com/networking/display/mlnxofedv461000/introduction" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Mellanox</a>), but it describes the packet, i.e., where packet bytes are stored, length of the packet, etc.</p>
<p>I will use the RX path as an example to illustrate why it's useful. Upon receiving a packet, the NIC translates the electronic/optical/radio signal on the wire to binary data bytes. Then the NIC needs to inform the OS that it has received something. In the old days, this was done by interrupts and OS would read bytes from a predefined location on the NIC to RAM. However, this is slow since 1) CPUs are required to participate in data transfer from the NIC to RAM 2) There could be lots of packets, thus lots of interrupts which could be too many to handle for CPU. Then <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Direct_memory_access" rel="nofollow noreferrer">DMA</a> came along and solved the first problem. Also, folks designed a poll mode driver (or hybrid mode, as in Linux <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_API" rel="nofollow noreferrer">NAPI</a>), so the CPU could be freed from interrupt handling and poll many packets at once, thus solving the second problem.</p>
<p>The NIC finishes signal translation to bytes and would like to do a DMA transfer to RAM. But before that, NIC needs to know where to DMA transfer to, as it can't randomly put data in RAM which CPU won't know where is and isn't secure.</p>
<p>So during initialization of the RX queue, NIC driver preallocates some packet buffer, as well as an array of packet descriptors. It initializes each packet descriptor according to the NIC definition.</p>
<p>Below is the convention used by the Intel XL710 NIC (names have been simplified for better understanding):</p>
<p><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/UXgrm.png" alt="XL710 RX read Descriptor" />
<img src="https://i.sstatic.net/KyaHw.png" alt="XL710 Rx Writeback Descriptor" /></p>
<pre><code>/*
   Rx descriptor used by XL710 is filled by both driver and NIC,
 * but at different stage of operations. Thus to save space, it's
 * defined as a union of read (by NIC) and writeback (by NIC).
 *
 * It must follow the description from the data sheet table above.
 *
 * __leXX below means little endian XX bit field.
 * The endianness and length has to be explicit, the NIC can be used by different CPU with different word size and endianness.
 */

union rx_desc {
    struct {
        __le64 pkt_addr; /* Packet buffer address, points to a free packet buffer in packet_buffer_pool */
        __le64 hdr_addr; /* Header buffer address, normally isn't used */
    } read; /* initialized by driver */

    struct {
        struct {
            struct {
                union {
                    __le16 mirroring_status;
                    __le16 fcoe_ctx_id;
                } mirr_fcoe;
                __le16 l2tag1;
            } lo_dword;
            union {
                __le32 rss; /* RSS Hash */
                __le32 fd_id; /* Flow director filter id */
                __le32 fcoe_param; /* FCoE DDP Context id */
            } hi_dword;
        } qword0;
        struct {
            /* ext status/error/pktype/length */
            __le64 status_error_len;
        } qword1;
    } wb;  /* writeback by NIC */
};

/*
 * Rx Queue defines a circular ring of Rx descriptors
 */
struct rx_queue {
    volatile rx_desc rx_ring[RING_SIZE]; /* RX ring of descriptors */
    struct packet_buffer_pool *pool;     /* packet pool */
    struct packet_buffer *pkt_addr_backup; /* save a copy of packet buffer address for writeback descriptor reuse */
....
}
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/tx3Ul.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/tx3Ul.png" alt="RX Descriptor Data Structure" /></a></p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/Ayx8U.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/Ayx8U.png" alt="Enter image description here" /></a></p>
<ol>
<li><p>The driver allocates some number of packet buffer in RAM (stored in the packet_buffer_pool data structure).</p>
<pre><code>pool = alloc_packet_buffer_pool(buffer_size=2048, num_buffer=512);
</code></pre>
</li>
<li><p>Driver put each packet buffer's address in the descriptor field, like</p>
<pre><code>rx_ring[i]-&gt;read.pkt_addr = pool.get_free_buffer();
</code></pre>
</li>
<li><p>The driver tells the NIC the starting location of rx_ring, its length and head/tail. So the NIC would know which descriptors are free (thus packet buffer pointed by those descriptors are free). This process is done by the driver writing this information into NIC registers (fixed, and it could be found in the NIC datasheet).</p>
<pre><code>rx_ring_addr_reg = &amp;rx_ring;
rx_ring_len_reg = sizeof(rx_ring);
rx_ring_head = 0; /* Meaning all free at start */
/* rx_ring_tail is a register in the NIC as the NIC updates it */
</code></pre>
</li>
<li><p>Now the NIC knows that descriptors, rx_ring[{x,y,z}], are free and {x,y,z}.pkt_addr could be put new packet data. It goes ahead and DMA transfers the new packets into {x,y,z}.pkt_addr. In the meantime, NIC could preprocess (offload) the packet processing (like check sum validation, and extracting the VLAN tag), so it would also need some place to leave those info for software. Here, descriptors are reused for this purpose on <em>writeback</em> (see the second struct in descriptor union). Then the NIC advances the rx_ring head pointer offset, indicating a new descriptor has been written back by the NIC. [A gotcha here is that, since descriptors are reused for preprocessing results, the driver has to save {x,y,z}.pkt_addr in a backup data structure].</p>
<pre><code>/* The below is done in hardware and is shown just for illustration purposes */
if (rx_ring_head != rx_ring_tail) { /* ring not full */
    copy(rx_ring[rx_ring_head].read.pkt_addr, raw_packet_data);
    result = do_offload_procesing();

    if (pre_processing(raw_packet_data) &amp; BAD_CHECKSUM))
        rx_ring[rx_ring_head].writeback.qword1.stats_error_len |= RX_BAD_CHECKSUM_ERROR;
    rx_ring_head++; /* Actually the driver sets a Descriptor done indication flag */
                    /* along in the writeback descriptor, so the driver can figure out */
                    /* the current HEAD, thus saving a PCIe write message */
}
</code></pre>
</li>
<li><p>The driver reads the new head pointer offset and found {x,y,z} are with the new packets. It would read off the packet from pkt_addr_backup[{x,y,z}] and the related preprocessing result.</p>
</li>
<li><p>When the upper layer software is done with the packets, {x,y,z} would be put back into rx_ring and the ring head pointer would be updated to indicate free descriptors.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>This concludes the RX path. The TX path is pretty much the reverse: The upper layer produces a packet, the driver copies packet data into packet_buffer_pool and let tx_ring[x].buffer_addr point to it. The driver also prepares some TX offload flags (such as hardware checksumming, TSO) in the TX descriptor. The NIC reads the TX descriptor and DMA tx_ring[x].buffer_addr from RAM to the NIC.</p>
<p>This information normally appears in the NIC datasheet, such as the <a href="http://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/embedded/products/networking/xl710-10-40-controller-datasheet.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Intel XL710 xl710-10-40-controller datasheet</a>, Chapter 8.3 &amp; 8.4 LAN RX/TX Data Path.</p>
<p>Also, you can check the open source driver code (either the Linux kernel or some user space library, like DPDK PMD), which would contain descriptor struct definition.</p>
<p>For the question regarding the Realtek driver:
Yes, those bits are NIC-specific. A hint is lines like</p>
<pre><code>    desc-&gt;opts1 = cpu_to_le32(DescOwn | RingEnd | cp-&gt;rx_buf_sz);
</code></pre>
<p>DescOwn is a bit flag which, by setting it, tells the NIC that it now owns this descriptor and the associated buffer. Also it needs to convert from CPU endianness (it might be a <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PowerPC" rel="nofollow noreferrer">PowerPC</a> CPU, which is <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Endianness#Big-endian" rel="nofollow noreferrer">big-endian</a> (BE)) to <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Endianness#Little-endian" rel="nofollow noreferrer">little-endian</a> that the NIC agrees to understand.</p>
<p>You can find the relevant information in <em><a href="http://realtek.info/pdf/rtl8139cp.pdf" rel="nofollow noreferrer">RTL8139C(L)+. 3.3 V single-chip fast Ethernet controller with power management</a></em> (e.g., page 70 for DescOwn), though it's not as thorough as XL710, but at least contains all register/descriptor information.</p>
<p>The NIC descriptor is a very vendor-dependent definition. As shown above, Intel's NIC descriptor uses the <em>same</em> RX descriptor ring to provide NIC buffers to write to, and for the NIC to write back the RX information. There are other implementations, like split RX submission/completion queue (more prevalent in <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NVM_Express" rel="nofollow noreferrer">NVMe</a> technology). For instance, some of <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Broadcom_Inc." rel="nofollow noreferrer">Broadcom</a>'s NICs have a single submission ring (to give the buffer to the NIC) and multiple completion rings. It's designed for the NIC decide and put packets in different rings for, e.g., different traffic class priority, so that the driver can get most important packets first.</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/gC0ZG.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/gC0ZG.png" alt="Enter image description here" /></a> (From BCM5756M NIC Programmer’s Guide)</p>
<p>Intel usually makes the NIC datasheet open to public download, while other vendors might disclose to <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Original_design_manufacturer" rel="nofollow noreferrer">ODMs</a> only. A very brief summary of Tx/Rx flow is described in their <a href="https://www.intel.com/content/dam/www/public/us/en/documents/datasheets/82599-10-gbe-controller-datasheet.pdf" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Intel 82599</a> family datasheet, section <em>1.8 Architecture and Basic Operations</em>.</p>

Q: How to send data from input using asp-route attribute? A: <p>If you pass <code>username</code> and <code>password</code> using <code>asp-route-{value}</code>, it will show these information in the url which is not recommended.</p>

<p>If you just want to pass the two input values from view to controller, you need to modify <code>name</code> attribute of input to match the action parameters.For example:</p>

<p>View:</p>

<pre><code>&lt;form method="post" asp-controller="Members" asp-action="LoginUser"&gt;
    &lt;span&gt;&lt;label&gt;&lt;b&gt;Korisnik :&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/label&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;input type="text" name="username" id="korisnik" size="40" height="25" /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;span&gt;&lt;label&gt;&lt;b&gt;Lozinka :&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/label&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;input type="password" name="password" id="lozinka" size="40" height="25" /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;button type="submit" title="Login" class="btnLogin"&gt;&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;
</code></pre>

<p>Action:</p>

<pre><code>[HttpPost]
public async Task&lt;IActionResult&gt; LoginUser(string username,string password)
</code></pre>

Q: Fullscreen overlay over desktop A: <p>UNTESTED</p>

<p>It sounds like your problem is clicking.  In order to pass through your mouse messages, have you considered intercepting the mouse click on your top window, (assuming this has been set to be a transparent window), then hiding your window, fire the same mouse message using the user32 import SendMessage, and then showing your window again?  If you don't want to hide your window, you can probably just call SendMessage directly to the various other window handles.</p>

<p>The following link shows how to cycle through the windows on the desktop of your application:</p>

<p><a href="http://support.microsoft.com/kb/183009" rel="nofollow">http://support.microsoft.com/kb/183009</a></p>

<p>I think you can also override the CreateParams if you have already made your form transparent.  Doing so may allow the mouse events to pass through.</p>

<pre><code>// This may be even simpler
protected override CreateParams CreateParams
{
    get
    {
        CreateParams createParams = base.CreateParams;
        createParams.ExStyle |= 0x00000020; // WS_EX_TRANSPARENT

        return createParams;
    }
}
</code></pre>

<p>You can make the form itself transparent in newer windows forms like:</p>

<pre><code>public void MakeSeeThru()
{
   frmTransparentForm.Opacity = 0.83;
}
</code></pre>

<p>The above only works on Form.  To do this on child controls takes more work.  First you have to set the style to support transparent back color.</p>

<pre><code>public TransparentControl()
{
    SetStyle(ControlStyles.SupportsTransparentBackColor, true);
    this.BackColor = Color.Transparent.
}
</code></pre>

<p>If the control is transparent, this does not stop you from overriding the OnPaintBackground and OnPaint methods and do your custom drawing if you like.  It just draws with the default as a see-through background.</p>

<p>I've done this before and there is a bit of a blur if this moves around.  However if the entire thing is a huge transparent form that covers your desktop there should be no motion blur.</p>

<p>If that has performance problems and you want to custom draw the desktop, you can grab a screenshot like:</p>

<pre><code>ScreenCapture sc = new ScreenCapture();
// capture entire screen, and save it to a file
Image img = sc.CaptureScreen();
</code></pre>

<p>You will need to hide your form first though, so that will likely cause a flicker.</p>
